</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training.</span>
The 3D reconstruction loss used in Eqs. (11) to (13) follows the implementation in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which includes supervision on ray directions, camera poses, depth maps, point maps, etc.
For calibration token implementation, we insert <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math> tokens to each transformer encoder layer in the image encoder and alternating attention model, except the first <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}=12</annotation></semantics></math> layers of the image encoder, as more calibration tokens yield relatively less performance gain (Fig. 6).
Ablation in <a href="#S6.T3" title="In 6 Experimental Details ‣ Fisheye3R: Adapting Unified 3D Feed-Forward Foundation Models to Fisheye Lenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that excluding the first 12 layers from calibration would not degrade the performance significantly.
For each model, we freeze its original backbone and initialize calibration tokens with values sampled from a normal distribution with mean zero and standard deviation 1e-6.
The calibration tokens are then trained for <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> iterations by AdamW optimizer with a learning rate gradually decaying from 1e-5 to 1e-7.
Gradient checkpoint is enabled on the encoders to reduce the GPU memory consumption during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span>
We generally follow the protocols of prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> for 3D reconstruction evaluation.
Specifically, predicted depth maps are aligned to ground truth by applying a scale and shift per sequence.
The predicted point maps are aligned to the ground truth using Umeyama algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> to estimate a similarity transformation, followed by a refinement step by Iterative Closest Point (ICP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
Then, the point cloud metrics are computed based on the mean of nearest-neighbor distances between the predicted and ground-truth points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Camera type classification.</span>
The camera type classifier is implemented as a simple linear model (i.e., logistic regression), which is pre-trained prior to learning the calibration tokens and converges in one minute with no GPU, achieving 99.9%+ accuracy on the test datasets.
This indicates that distinguishing fisheye from perspective images is relatively straightforward from their class embeddings, thanks to their distinct distortion patterns.
A more complex classifier could potentially yield further improvements, but given that the classification accuracy has already saturated on our test sets, we adopt a simple linear classifier for efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span>
The entire framework is implemented in PyTorch, with the masked attention mechanism realized by the <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_path ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">attn_mask</span> argument in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_path ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention()</span> function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware configurations.</span>
The models are trained on a machine with 4 NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs (48GiB each) and 56 dual Intel Xeon Gold 6258R CPU cores.</p>
</div>
